<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Records of the Three Kingdoms</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sanguo zhi, Sanguozhi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Records of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> (Sanguo zhi) is the official history of the Three Kingdoms period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> (220–280 CE), written by Chen Shou in the late third century. Its accounts of the states of Wei, Shu and Wu, expanded by Pei Songzhi's fifth-century commentary, are the main source for the period and for the novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a>. Its chapter on the eastern peoples is the earliest detailed description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms.jpg" alt="Records of the Three Kingdoms" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 猫猫的日记本 (Cat's diary) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Fragment_of_Biography_of_Bu_Zhi_History_Books_of_Three_Kingdoms_01_2012-12.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
